  /* The thumbnail: the same plate, turned into a column flush to the card's
     left edge instead of a band across its top. No flanks — there is not
     enough width left for the shelf illusion once the plate is this narrow —
     and the specimen keeps its own small white mat rather than bleeding to
     the edge, so it still reads as *paper sitting on a wash* rather than the
     wash itself. The source masthead hides too: the publisher's name is
     already said once, in the text beside this, and repeating it in a mark
     this small was the name competing with itself. */
  /* Side-by-side, and only where there is room for it. Below the breakpoint
     this whole block stops applying and a compact card falls back to the base
     card's own layout — plate stacked on top at full width, flanks and
     masthead back, specimen at its 58% — which is the right shape on a narrow
     screen and costs no un-setting to reach. 10rem of a 335px card was half
     the width, and the title lost to it.

     The ratio is set by what sits *inside* the plate, not by the plate. A
     video poster is 16:9 and `.dcard__link-image` paints it `cover`, so any
     box narrower than 16:9 crops its sides — which is what three earlier cuts
     all did, each less badly than the last. Square (7rem × 7.25rem) took half
     the width off a poster; 10rem × 4:3 fixed the gross distortion but still
     cut the sheet off after three lines; 13rem × 4:3 was legible and still
     clipped a poster to 1.46:1, losing the left of "Mark" and the right of the
     bookmark.

     The padding is equal on both axes and so does not preserve a ratio: the
     plate loses its own mat each way and the specimen's on top, and the picture
     ends up the remainder. Sized so that remainder is exactly 160 × 90.

     The height is set by the text beside it, not chosen: the body needs 120px
     at its tallest (eyebrow, title, two clamped lines, padding), and the plate
     was standing 152px tall and setting the row — up to 53px of white space
     under a description that had already stopped. 190 × 120 at 19/12 with 8px
     and 7px mats lands the picture on 160 × 90, so the poster stays whole and
     the card stops being taller than it has anything to put in. */
  @media (min-width: 561px) {
